# Backend Naming Conventions

## Database — always snake_case

All identifiers that touch the database **must** use `snake_case`:

- SQL column names, table names, index names, constraint names
- TypeScript interface/object properties that map to DB columns or are stored as JSONB (e.g. `RecognitionResultItem`, `MatchedPlace`, `ExtractedPlace`, `ExtractedPlaceWithMatch`)
- Zod schema field names for request/response bodies that mirror DB rows

```ts
// ✅ correct
interface MatchedPlace {
  google_place_id: string;
  first_photo_name: string | null;
  confidence_score: number;
}

// ❌ wrong — camelCase must not appear in DB-bound types
interface MatchedPlace {
  googlePlaceId: string;
  firstPhotoName: string | null;
  confidenceScore: number;
}
```

## TypeScript application code — camelCase

Internal variables, function parameters, class properties, and non-DB interfaces
follow standard TypeScript camelCase:

```ts
const ownerUserId = params.ownerUserId;
async function getPlaceDetails(placeId: string) { … }
```

## Quick reference

| Layer | Convention |
|---|---|
| DB columns / SQL | `snake_case` |
| JSONB-stored object keys | `snake_case` |
| API response bodies (mirrors DB row) | `snake_case` |
| TypeScript vars / params / methods | `camelCase` |
| TypeScript types / interfaces | `PascalCase` |
